concepts/cartesian-metric-tensor.content:
Since we have something called the “Minkowski Metric Tensor”, which is the diagonal matrix \operatorname{Diag}(-1,1,1,1) it would seem reasonable to have something simpler to compare it to, before we move on to more complex things like the “Polar Metric Tensor” or the “Spherical Metric Tensor”.
So, I introduce, the Cartesian Metric Tensor, which is the Identity matrix; I=\operatorname{Diag}(1,1,1)
The Cartesian Metric Tensor is the characteristic of the length of a differential path element in Cartesian coordinates
\\&amp;=\overset\rightarrow {d(x,y,z)} (I) d(x,y,z)\downarrow
\\&amp;=\Pmatrix{dx&amp;dy&amp;dz}\Pmatrix{1&amp;0&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;0&amp;1}\Pmatrix{dx\\dy\\dz}
}$$

concepts/local-and-global-mapping.content:
The Lorentz Transformations are a Global Mapping.  They take all of the events in space and time as the Domain and map them to all events in space and time as the Range.  It might be worth applying a Zoom-Out Principle describing the perspectival changes of Lorentz Transformations to compare to the Zoom-In Principle applied in modeling physical changes due to laws of mechanics.
On the other hand, the metric tensor g_{\mu\nu}
is a local mapping, taking, for example a tiny interval, (d\tau, dr, d\phi)  and mapping it to (dt,dx,dy) in such a way that either the Minkowski Metric is conserved, or that a Lagrangian, Action, and Euler-Lagrange Equations is conserved.

concepts/metric-isoclines.content:
Compare to Parallel World Lines
Metric Isoclines in Polar and Rectangular Coordinates
Consider the Polar to Rectangular Transformation
The transformation of interest converts from polar coordinates to rectangular coordinates .
With this in mind, we can describe four metric isoclines

Horizontal lines:  \Align{y&amp;=\text{constant}\\\Rightarrow  dy &amp;= 0}
Vertical Lines:  \Align{x&amp;=\text{constant}\\\Rightarrow dx&amp;=0}
Circles around the origin:  \Align{r&amp;=\text{constant}\\\Rightarrow  dr &amp;= 0}
Rays pointing from the center: \Align{\phi&amp;=\text{constant}\\\Rightarrow  d\phi &amp;= 0}
